commit 782c1de1a0e79210d2ac87fa0aa96ba76bc78a8d
Author:     Jonathan Saggau <jsaggau@enharmonichq.com>
AuthorDate: Mon Aug 31 13:39:34 2026 -0400
Commit:     Jonathan Saggau <jsaggau@enharmonichq.com>
CommitDate: Mon Aug 31 13:39:34 2026 -0400

    notifications 4.1 + versions 4.2: the daemon becomes a relay, and versions warn without refusing
    
    4.1. The daemon now advertises tools.listChanged (the runner's default
    NotificationOptions is overridden at the one externally reachable seam)
    and pushes notifications/tools/list_changed to every downstream client
    it has seen, whenever an upstream connects, breaks, or announces its own
    listChanged (tapped via the client session's message_handler). Events
    debounce for half a second so a reconnect burst becomes one
    notification. The downstream registry keeps the latest per-connection
    ServerSession — the standalone channel is connection-scoped and stable —
    and a client that never drains its GET stream is skipped on a one-second
    timeout rather than wedging the broadcaster. This closes the phase-1
    panel's highest-ranked deferral: a client that listed during an Xcode
    outage cached 29 tools and was never told the other 21 came back.
    
    4.2. A per-server 'version' field records the compatible
    serverInfo.version. Exact-string comparison at connect, advisory only:
    the surface keeps serving. In-band, the mismatch is appended to the
    instructions, which the SDK re-reads at every new session's initialize —
    each fresh model is told, with no new mechanism. For the human, one log
    line per distinct (upstream, expected, found), persisted to
    version-mismatches.json so a launchd restart does not re-raise it — the
    2026-08-30 lesson is that a warning per reconnect tick is twenty-one
    modals in an evening. The note suggests the collision comparison and
    never auto-runs it, per Jonathan.
    
    stub_mcp_server grew --emit-list-changed-on-call for a deterministic
    relay trigger. Watched red: the daemon initially died on the unknown
    'version' field, and the capability, mismatch, persistence and relay
    assertions all failed before the implementation.

