     5	# ///
     6	"""xcode-mcp-front daemon.
     7	
     8	Problem: Xcode's MCP-connection "Allow" prompt is keyed per connecting PID. Every
     9	separate process that execs `xcrun mcpbridge` (one per kicker node, one per Claude
    10	Code / Codex session) makes Xcode ask again. kicker runs many concurrent nodes, so
    11	that's many concurrent popups for the same underlying tool.
    12	
    13	Fix: hold exactly ONE `xcrun mcpbridge` child process for the whole life of this
    14	daemon, and front it with a Streamable HTTP MCP server. Every downstream client
    15	(kicker nodes, Claude Code, Codex — anything that can speak MCP-over-HTTP) points
    16	at this daemon's URL instead of spawning mcpbridge itself. Xcode approves ONE PID,
    17	once, and stays approved as long as this daemon (and its mcpbridge child) keep running.
    18	
    19	Runs in one of two modes, chosen by which env vars are set (see below):
    20	  - SINGLE-upstream (default, what's been running since 2026-08-14): fronts just
    21	    one upstream (normally mcpbridge). Dumb passthrough — tool names forwarded
    22	    unprefixed, exactly as the upstream advertises them. This is the deployed
    23	    xcode-mcp-front daemon; nothing about its behavior changes in this mode.
    24	  - MULTI-upstream (XCODE_MCP_FRONT_UPSTREAMS set): fronts several upstreams
    25	    behind the SAME endpoint, tool names prefixed per-upstream
    26	    (`<name>__<tool>`, e.g. `xcode__BuildProject`, `drews__run_project_unmonitored`)
    27	    so two upstreams that happen to share a tool name can't collide or shadow
    28	    each other. Built for exactly one reason: Drew's xcode-mcp
    29	    (`drews-xcode-mcp`) has no approval-dialog friction on its own — a client can
    30	    already spawn `uvx drews-xcode-mcp` directly with zero setup, no wrapper
    31	    needed — so it never got its own standalone daemon (built, verified, then
    32	    torn back down, 2026-08-14 — see git history). But "one endpoint with BOTH
    33	    Xcode tools available, prefixed so there's no confusion" is a real,
    34	    different thing worth having alongside the single-upstream daemon, not
    35	    instead of it. Both modes share every line of connection/click/reconnect
    36	    logic below via the Upstream class — no duplicated logic between them.
    37	
    38	This is a dumb passthrough, not a real aggregator: it forwards list_tools/call_tool
    39	to the relevant upstream ClientSession(s) and returns whatever comes back. It does
    40	not interpret tool semantics. tools/call requests to a given upstream are
    41	serialized through that upstream's own lock; tools/list requests — client drains
    42	and the heartbeat alike — run WITHOUT the lock on a session snapshot, because the
    43	MCP session multiplexes concurrent requests by id and a list must never wait
    44	behind a long-running build (measured by the phase-1 panel: a client connecting
    45	during one upstream's slow call got no tool list at all until it finished).
    46	Different upstreams are fully independent of each other.
    47	
    48	VALIDATED 2026-08-14 (all live, against a real Xcode):
    49	- One connection tolerates many sequential calls of different shapes, no reconnect
    50	  needed (spike.py, then a real multi-call session through this daemon).
    51	- Quitting Xcode does NOT crash this process — it keeps serving HTTP, but the
    52	  mcpbridge child dies and every call after that fails clean with "Connection
    53	  closed".
    54	- The approval prompt's PID (confirmed from the actual dialog, not assumed) is
    55	  THIS daemon's own PID, not the ephemeral mcpbridge child's — so as long as this
    56	  process keeps running, reconnecting mcpbridge in a loop should NOT need a fresh
    57	  approval each time, only the very first connection (or after a genuine
    58	  permission reset) does.
    59	- The approval prompt is requested LAZILY, on the first list_tools call, not at
    60	  the initial connect — the per-upstream reconnect loop polls for it (and
    61	  clicks it) continuously in the connected steady-state too, not just while
    62	  reconnecting.
    63	- Xcode's approval dialogs do NOT stack — only one shows at a time, and a stale
    64	  unanswered one blocks the next (including this daemon's own) from appearing.
    65	  The click logic reads each dialog's own PID: clicks Allow for our own live
    66	  pid, clicks Don't Allow for a dead pid (nobody's waiting on it — a plain
    67	  os.kill(pid,0) liveness check, no PID-history file needed), leaves any other
    68	  live pid's dialog strictly alone.
    69	- Drew's xcode-mcp (drews-xcode-mcp) has NO approval-dialog behavior at all —
    70	  folder-allowlist auth (XCODEMCP_ALLOWED_FOLDERS / --allowed) instead of a
    71	  live per-PID popup. Its Upstream instance just never finds a dialog to click;
    72	  harmless no-op, no special-casing needed.
    73	
    74	Env overrides — SINGLE-upstream mode (default):
    75	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_UPSTREAM_CMD    default "xcrun"
    76	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_UPSTREAM_ARGS   default "mcpbridge" (space-separated)
    77	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_REQUIRE_XCODE   default "1" — wait for Xcode.app before connecting.
    78	                                   Set "0" for an upstream (like drews-xcode-mcp)
    79	                                   that doesn't need a live Xcode process at all.
    80	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_AUTO_ALLOW      default "1" — click-Allow-automatically. Off
    81	                                   switch for anyone who'd rather approve by hand.
    82	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_SERVER_NAME     default "xcode-mcp-front"
    83	
    84	Env overrides — MULTI-upstream mode (mutually exclusive with the single-upstream
    85	UPSTREAM_CMD/ARGS/REQUIRE_XCODE vars above — set THIS instead):
    86	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_MCP_INFO        path to a _mcp_info.json in the Claude Code shape
    87	                                   ({"mcpServers": {"<name>": {"command", "args"}}}),
    88	                                   plus per-server "prefix" (default "<name>__") and
    89	                                   "quirks" (["require_xcode"] for an upstream that
    90	                                   needs a live Xcode). See mcp_config.py, which
    91	                                   validates it and REJECTS fields the daemon does
    92	                                   not pass through (env, cwd, url, ...) by name.
    93	                                   Tool names are prefixed in the merged tool list
    94	                                   and un-prefixed again when routing a call back.
    95	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_UPSTREAMS       REPLACED by the file above; setting it is a
    96	                                   startup error naming the replacement. The old
    97	                                   colon/comma format corrupted a command path
    98	                                   containing ':' and an argument containing ','.
    99	
   100	Env overrides — both modes:
   101	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_HOST             default "127.0.0.1" (do not bind wider — no auth layer)
   102	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_PORT             default "8765"
   103	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_XCODE_APP_PATH   default "/Applications/Xcode.app" — the reconnect
   104	                                    loop matches THIS exact binary path, not a bare
   105	                                    process name (Xcode.app and Xcode-beta.app share
   106	                                    both their process name and bundle ID, so only
   107	                                    the full path tells them apart — confirmed live)
   108	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_RECONNECT_POLL_S default "5" (seconds between reconnect attempts)
   109	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_S default "15" (per-attempt connect timeout, so a
   110	                                    stuck approval dialog can't hang a whole attempt
   111	                                    forever — it just times out and the next tick
   112	                                    tries the click again)
   113	"""
   114	
   115	import asyncio
   116	import contextlib
   117	import functools
   118	import json
   119	import logging
   120	import os
   121	import re
   122	import subprocess
   123	import time
   124	
   125	import anyio
   126	import uvicorn
   127	
   128	import mcp_config
   129	from mcp import types
   130	from mcp.client.session import ClientSession
   131	from mcp.client.stdio import StdioServerParameters, get_default_environment, stdio_client
   132	from mcp.shared.exceptions import MCPError
   133	from mcp.server.lowlevel import Server
   134	from mcp.server.lowlevel.server import NotificationOptions, ServerRequestContext
   135	
   136	
   137	def _load_config_file() -> None:
   138	    """Reached via a wrapped .app under launchd in real deployment, which does
   139	    NOT reliably forward a launchd plist's EnvironmentVariables into the
   140	    launched app's process (a LaunchServices quirk, separate from the TCC one
   141	    this tool is already built around). A config file sidesteps that entirely:
   142	    it's read directly off disk, so it works regardless of how this process was
   143	    launched. Real env vars still win if both are set (checked with setdefault,
   144	    same layering as ollama-watch's config convention elsewhere in this repo)."""
   145	    conf = os.path.join(os.environ.get("XCODE_MCP_FRONT_HOME", os.path.expanduser("~/.xcode-mcp-front")), "config")
   146	    if not os.path.isfile(conf):
   147	        return
   148	    with open(conf) as f:
   149	        for line in f:
   150	            line = line.strip()
   151	            if not line or line.startswith("#") or "=" not in line:
   152	                continue
   153	            key, _, value = line.partition("=")
   154	            os.environ.setdefault(key.strip(), value.strip().strip("'\""))
   155	
   156	
   157	_load_config_file()
   158	
   159	HOST = os.environ.get("XCODE_MCP_FRONT_HOST", "127.0.0.1")
   160	PORT = int(os.environ.get("XCODE_MCP_FRONT_PORT", "8765"))
   161	AUTO_ALLOW = os.environ.get("XCODE_MCP_FRONT_AUTO_ALLOW", "1") == "1"
   162	
   163	# Both /Applications/Xcode.app and /Applications/Xcode-beta.app share the exact
   164	# same CFBundleExecutable ("Xcode") AND the same CFBundleIdentifier
   165	# (com.apple.dt.Xcode) - confirmed live, 2026-08-14. Neither a bare process-name
   166	# match nor a bundle-ID match can tell them apart. Only the executable's full
   167	# path differs, so that's what this matches on - anchored and exact, not a
   168	# substring, so it can't accidentally match the beta path either.
   169	XCODE_APP_PATH = os.environ.get("XCODE_MCP_FRONT_XCODE_APP_PATH", "/Applications/Xcode.app")
   170	XCODE_BINARY_PATH = f"{XCODE_APP_PATH.rstrip('/')}/Contents/MacOS/Xcode"
   171	RECONNECT_POLL_SECONDS = float(os.environ.get("XCODE_MCP_FRONT_RECONNECT_POLL_S", "5"))
   172	CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = float(os.environ.get("XCODE_MCP_FRONT_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_S", "15"))
   173	# Generous on purpose - a real Xcode build_project call can legitimately run
   174	# for minutes. The point isn't to bound normal work, it's to make sure NO call
   175	# can wedge the daemon forever (found live, 2026-08-14: with no timeout at all,
   176	# one genuinely hung upstream call held the connection lock for 2+ minutes and
   177	# counting, wedging every future call to that upstream, single-upstream or
   178	# combined, until manually restarted). Raise this if a real workload needs
   179	# longer than the default.
   180	#
   181	# Default corrected to 600s (was 120s) by convocation review, 2026-08-14: 120s
   182	# contradicted this comment's own "can legitimately run for minutes" — a real
   183	# clean/first build commonly runs past 2 minutes, so the old default silently
   184	# killed and dropped exactly the workload it claimed to tolerate, then
   185	# reconnected and reported "not connected right now, retry shortly" — which is
   186	# actively misleading once this fires, since the build was killed mid-flight,
   187	# not merely delayed; a retry starts it over, it doesn't resume it.
   188	CALL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = float(os.environ.get("XCODE_MCP_FRONT_CALL_TIMEOUT_S", "600"))
   189	SERVER_NAME = os.environ.get("XCODE_MCP_FRONT_SERVER_NAME", "xcode-mcp-front")
   190	
   191	logging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format=f"%(asctime)s {SERVER_NAME} %(message)s")
   192	log = logging.getLogger(SERVER_NAME)
   193	
   194	
   195	# NOTE: no longer consulted by the click decision. It used to gate the "Don't Allow" branch
   196	# on the dialog's PID being dead, and that gate is what let a LIVE stranger's dialog block
   197	# the daemon forever. Kept because it is a correct, cheap liveness check and reads clearly
   198	# at a call site; delete it if nothing else picks it up.
   199	def _pid_is_alive(pid_str: str) -> bool:
   200	    """No history file needed: a dialog's PID is either us, or it's a live PID
   201	    that belongs to someone else's legitimate in-flight request (leave it
   202	    alone), or it's simply not in the process table anymore (nothing is
   203	    waiting on it — always safe to dismiss, regardless of whose it was).
   204	    Generalizes to every stale dialog, not just ones this exact daemon
   205	    happens to remember spawning itself, so it can never permanently
   206	    deadlock behind one it doesn't recognize."""
   207	    try:
   208	        os.kill(int(pid_str), 0)
   209	        return True
   210	    except ProcessLookupError:
   211	        return False
   212	    except (ValueError, PermissionError):
   213	        # ValueError: not a real pid, treat as "can't tell" -> leave it alone.
   214	        # PermissionError: pid exists but isn't ours to signal -> still alive.
   215	        return True
   216	
   217	
   218	def _xcode_is_running() -> bool:
   219	    try:
   220	        # -f matches the full command line; anchored so it can only match this
   221	        # exact binary path, not a substring of some other process's args.
   222	        return (
   223	            subprocess.run(["pgrep", "-f", f"^{XCODE_BINARY_PATH}$"], capture_output=True, timeout=5).returncode
   224	            == 0
   225	        )
   226	    except Exception:
   227	        # A broken check must never itself take the daemon down — treat "can't
   228	        # tell" as "not running" and just try again next poll.
   229	        return False
   230	
   231	
   232	async def _run_osascript(script: str) -> bytes:
   233	    """Raises on a nonzero exit instead of returning empty bytes. Found by
   234	    convocation review, 2026-08-14: with stderr sent to DEVNULL and no
   235	    returncode check, a TCC grant getting revoked or dying (the documented
   236	    failure mode after any edit to the ad-hoc-signed wrapper .app — new hash,
   237	    dead grant) made osascript exit nonzero with an error, which this
   238	    previously returned as plain b"" — indistinguishable from "no dialog
   239	    showing". The click loop would then silently stop working with nothing in
   240	    the log naming the actual cause. Now surfaces it: caller's except-block
   241	    already logs at warning level, so a dead grant shows up as a real,
   242	    diagnosable log line instead of a silent no-op.
   243	
   244	    Also found LIVE the same day, the hard way: a real System Events hang
   245	    (osascript stuck talking to it, unrelated to the return-code fix above —
   246	    System Events itself was wedged) piled up one leaked orphan osascript
   247	    process EVERY poll tick, forever, because `asyncio.wait_for` timing out
   248	    only abandons Python's own wait — it does NOT kill the underlying
   249	    subprocess. Confirmed live: 30+ orphaned osascript processes accumulated
   250	    in under 10 minutes before this was caught, and System Events itself
   251	    became unresponsive to even a basic query as a result. Now explicitly
   252	    kills the process on timeout instead of leaving it to run forever."""
   253	    proc = await asyncio.create_subprocess_exec(
   254	        "osascript",
   255	        "-e",
   256	        script,
   257	        stdout=asyncio.subprocess.PIPE,
   258	        stderr=asyncio.subprocess.PIPE,
   259	    )
   260	    try:
   261	        out, err = await asyncio.wait_for(proc.communicate(), timeout=5)
   262	    except asyncio.TimeoutError:
   263	        proc.kill()
   264	        await proc.wait()  # reap it — don't leave a zombie behind either
   265	        raise RuntimeError("osascript timed out after 5s (System Events may be unresponsive)")
   266	    if proc.returncode != 0:
   267	        raise RuntimeError(f"osascript exited {proc.returncode}: {err.decode(errors='replace').strip()}")
   268	    return out
   269	
   270	
   271	# THE CLICKER IS LOAD-BEARING AND MUST NOT BE "DESIGNED AWAY". Xcode's approval is keyed to
   272	# the CONNECTING PID, and a daemon restart always mints a new one, so no grant survives a
   273	# bounce — not a signed app bundle, not the "don't ask again for this agent binary" checkbox,
   274	# which only lasts until Xcode itself restarts. An unattended daemon therefore has to answer
   275	# its own prompt or it can never connect after any restart. Jonathan, 2026-08-30, correcting
   276	# a suggestion to remove it: "the daemons HAVE to depend on a clicker."
   277	#
   278	# That is why a defect in this function is not cosmetic. It is the only path to a working
   279	# connection, so a branch that silently declines to click takes the whole daemon down while
   280	# every log line still says "connected".
   281	
   282	
   283	# How long another process's approval dialog is left alone before we clear it. Only once we
   284	# have been blocked for this long is it fair to assume nobody is coming to answer it.
   285	# HOW LONG A FOREIGN DIALOG MAY BLOCK US BEFORE WE CLEAR IT. This MUST be comfortably less
   286	# than CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS or two daemons deadlock by arithmetic rather than by bad luck.
   287	#
   288	# It was 45 against a 15-second connect timeout. Xcode's approval dialogs do not stack, so when
   289	# both front daemons are up, each one's clicker finds the other's dialog, correctly classifies it
   290	# as foreign-and-alive, and waits out a grace period three times longer than the connection
   291	# attempt it is holding up. Its own prompt therefore never surfaces, its list_tools times out,
   292	# it tears the child down — which withdraws its request — and both sides repeat forever. That is
   293	# the "do two bridges interfere" question Jonathan asked: they do, and this constant is why.
   294	#
   295	# Clearing a live foreign dialog is not destructive. That process re-prompts on its next attempt,
   296	# which for these daemons is RECONNECT_POLL_SECONDS away, so two of them alternate and both get
   297	# through instead of neither.
   298	FOREIGN_DIALOG_GRACE_SECONDS = float(os.environ.get("XCODE_MCP_FRONT_FOREIGN_GRACE", "6"))
   299	
   300	# The relationship above is load-bearing, so it is checked rather than trusted to a comment. A
   301	# grace period at or beyond the connect timeout is a silent, permanent deadlock whenever a second
   302	# client exists, and it presents as "mcpbridge refuses to serve tools" — which is exactly how it
   303	# presented, for a night.
   304	if FOREIGN_DIALOG_GRACE_SECONDS >= CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS:
   305	    raise SystemExit(
   306	        "xcode-mcp-front: XCODE_MCP_FRONT_FOREIGN_GRACE (%.1fs) must be less than "
   307	        "XCODE_MCP_FRONT_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_S (%.1fs). A foreign approval dialog that outlives the "
   308	        "connection attempt it is blocking can never be cleared in time, so two daemons deadlock "
   309	        "and every symptom looks like Xcode refusing to serve tools."
   310	        % (FOREIGN_DIALOG_GRACE_SECONDS, CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS))
   311	
   312	# How long an upstream may make NO progress before we let launchd restart us. See
   313	# stall_watchdog(). Generous on purpose: a false positive restarts a healthy daemon.
   314	STALL_EXIT_SECONDS = float(os.environ.get("XCODE_MCP_FRONT_STALL_EXIT", "180"))
   315	
   316	# Structural ceiling on one tools/list drain, far above any real server's page count.
   317	# See Upstream.list_tools for why a wall-clock bound alone is not enough.
   318	LIST_MAX_PAGES = int(os.environ.get("XCODE_MCP_FRONT_LIST_MAX_PAGES", "200"))
   319	
   320	# How often to look for the approval dialog while a call is blocked waiting for it. Short,
   321	# because the window is the length of one blocked list_tools and a missed dialog costs the
   322	# whole connection attempt.
   323	ALLOW_CLICK_POLL_SECONDS = float(os.environ.get("XCODE_MCP_FRONT_CLICK_POLL", "1.5"))
   324	
   325	# Set the first time we see a foreign dialog while we are not connected; cleared whenever we
   326	# see no dialog or our own. Module-level rather than per-upstream because Xcode shows one
   327	# dialog at a time for the whole application, so this is genuinely global state.
   328	_foreign_dialog_first_seen: dict = {}
   329	
   330	
   331	async def _click_allow_if_present() -> bool:
   332	    """Best-effort, narrowly scoped: only ever touches a button whose title is
   333	    the exact literal string "Allow" or "Don't Allow", only in Xcode's own
   334	    process. This is the one dialog this tool exists to eat, not a general
   335	    click-any-prompt macro. Harmless no-op for an upstream (like
   336	    drews-xcode-mcp) that never triggers this dialog at all — it just never
   337	    finds anything to click.
   338	
   339	    Xcode's approval dialogs do NOT stack (confirmed live, 2026-08-14 — only
   340	    one is ever showing; a new connection attempt's own dialog stays hidden
   341	    behind whatever's currently up until that one is dismissed). So a single
   342	    stale, never-answered dialog can permanently block every future connection
   343	    attempt, including this daemon's own, unless something clears it.
   344	
   345	    The decision, once a dialog's own PID is read from its text:
   346	      - matches OUR current pid            -> click Allow
   347	      - doesn't match, and that pid is DEAD -> click Don't Allow (nobody's
   348	        waiting on it — always safe, regardless of whose it originally was;
   349	        no history-tracking needed, this can't deadlock behind a dialog it
   350	        doesn't "remember")

exec
/bin/zsh -lc "nl -ba /Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py | sed -n '351,700p'" in /Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/tests
 succeeded in 0ms:
   351	      - doesn't match, and that pid is ALIVE -> leave it alone; it's someone
   352	        else's legitimate in-flight request, not ours to approve OR deny
   353	    """
   354	    my_pid = str(os.getpid())
   355	    # Iterate every window, not just window 1 — found by convocation review,
   356	    # 2026-08-14: the approval dialog isn't guaranteed to be window 1 (e.g. a
   357	    # project window frontmost, or the dialog opening as window 2). Window 1
   358	    # only would silently never find the dialog and leave it unclicked
   359	    # forever. check-allow-window.sh already iterated all windows correctly;
   360	    # this production path didn't match it.
   361	    read_script = """
   362	tell application "System Events" to tell process "Xcode"
   363	  repeat with w in windows
   364	    if exists (button "Allow" of w) then
   365	      set winText to ""
   366	      try
   367	        set winText to (value of every static text of w) as string
   368	      end try
   369	      return winText
   370	    end if
   371	  end repeat
   372	  return ""
   373	end tell
   374	"""
   375	    try:
   376	        out = await _run_osascript(read_script)
   377	    except Exception as e:
   378	        log.warning("checking for an approval dialog failed (will retry, but if this repeats, check "
   379	                     "whether the wrapper .app's Accessibility/Automation grant is still valid): %s", e)
   380	        return False
   381	
   382	    text = out.decode(errors="replace")
   383	    if "PID: " not in text:
   384	        return False  # no dialog showing — the normal case most ticks
   385	
   386	    dialog_pid = text.split("PID: ", 1)[1].split()[0].strip()
   387	
   388	    if dialog_pid == my_pid:
   389	        _foreign_dialog_first_seen.pop("pid", None)
   390	        action = "Allow"
   391	    else:
   392	        # A GRACE PERIOD, BECAUSE CLEARING UNCONDITIONALLY MONOPOLISES XCODE. The previous
   393	        # version denied every foreign PID on sight. That unblocks us and starves everyone
   394	        # else: a legitimate agent's dialog gets Don't Allow, it retries, and it lands in the
   395	        # same denial loop forever, so this daemon owns Xcode and nothing else can use it.
   396	        # GhOST-OpenClaw caught it in peer review of the commit that introduced it.
   397	        #
   398	        # The bounded version keeps both properties. Somebody else's prompt is left alone
   399	        # while there is any chance they are about to answer it — which is the normal case,
   400	        # since a live agent's own approval usually resolves in seconds. Only once the SAME
   401	        # foreign dialog has been sitting there long enough to have stopped being anyone's
   402	        # in-flight request do we clear it so ours can surface. Nothing is destroyed either
   403	        # way: a denied agent re-prompts on its next attempt.
   404	        first = _foreign_dialog_first_seen.get("pid")
   405	        now = time.monotonic()
   406	        if first is None or first[0] != dialog_pid:
   407	            _foreign_dialog_first_seen["pid"] = (dialog_pid, now)
   408	            log.debug("a foreign approval dialog (pid %s) is in the way; leaving it for %.0fs "
   409	                      "in case its own agent answers it", dialog_pid, FOREIGN_DIALOG_GRACE_SECONDS)
   410	            return False
   411	        if now - first[1] < FOREIGN_DIALOG_GRACE_SECONDS:
   412	            return False
   413	        log.info("foreign approval dialog for pid %s has blocked us for %.0fs — clearing it so "
   414	                 "our own prompt can surface; that process re-prompts on its next attempt",
   415	                 dialog_pid, now - first[1])
   416	        # ANYTHING ELSE IN FRONT OF OURS GETS CLEARED, live or dead. This used to leave a
   417	        # LIVE unfamiliar PID's dialog strictly alone, out of politeness to another agent's
   418	        # in-flight request. That politeness is unaffordable here: Xcode's dialogs do not
   419	        # stack, so a single stranger's unanswered prompt sits in front of ours forever and
   420	        # the daemon can never connect again — which is exactly what a leftover
   421	        # "Allow 'Codex' to access Xcode?" from a convocation run did on 2026-08-30, and
   422	        # what the two front daemons did to each other for the sixteen days before that.
   423	        #
   424	        # Denying is not destructive and that is what makes this safe. A denied agent is
   425	        # told no for one connection attempt and re-prompts on its next one, by which time
   426	        # ours is out of the way. Jonathan, 2026-08-30: "I don't see why this needs
   427	        # exclusive access. Just run it saying with PID you care about or whatever?"
   428	        #
   429	        # The sibling branch above still matters and is not redundant: two front daemons
   430	        # denying each other would ping-pong, each clearing the other's prompt on every
   431	        # retry. Family gets Allow, everyone else gets out of the way.
   432	        action = "Don’t Allow"
   433	    click_script = f"""
   434	tell application "System Events" to tell process "Xcode"
   435	  repeat with w in windows
   436	    if exists (button "{action}" of w) then
   437	      click (button "{action}" of w)
   438	      return "clicked"
   439	    end if
   440	  end repeat
   441	  return "gone"
   442	end tell
   443	"""
   444	    try:
   445	        out2 = await _run_osascript(click_script)
   446	    except Exception as e:
   447	        log.warning("clicking the approval dialog failed (will retry, but if this repeats, check "
   448	                     "whether the wrapper .app's Accessibility/Automation grant is still valid): %s", e)
   449	        return False
   450	
   451	    clicked = b"clicked" in out2
   452	    if clicked and action == "Allow":
   453	        log.info("clicked Allow for our own connection-approval prompt (pid %s)", my_pid)
   454	    elif clicked:
   455	        log.info("cleared an approval dialog for pid %s (not ours, not a sibling) so ours can "
   456	                 "surface — it re-prompts on its next attempt", dialog_pid)
   457	    return clicked and action == "Allow"
